", "Open seat with a term-limited incumbent removes any incumbency effect", "2026 headlines show Democratic energy only in the Little Rock-area House district, not statewide"], "rationale": "Arkansas's deep Republican lean is the strongest available evidence, and nothing in the polling record, expert ratings, fundraising, or news flow points toward a statewide presidential shift; the open field keeps uncertainty wide but leaves the expected margin at the state's fundamentals.", "watch": "Once actual nominees emerge and Arkansas-specific polling appears, a nationally competitive Democratic nominee or a divisive Republican could tighten the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Arkansas remains one of the most Republican-leaning states on the presidential map, and the 2028 race there is an open contest with the incumbent term-limited. No general-election polls, expert ratings, or prediction-market prices are available for this race, so the forecast rests on the state's partisan lean of roughly fifteen points toward Republicans. Fundraising and outside spending data are likewise absent for both generic nominees.\n\nThe headline flow around Arkansas politics concerns downballot contests, including a competitive Democratic effort in the Little Rock-based House district and early chatter about the 2028 Republican primary, none of which points to a statewide presidential shift. With no evidence to move off the state's fundamentals, the race rates as Likely Republican, with wide uncertainty until actual nominees and polling emerge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0496, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0891, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0911, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "20